Only on Dlvl2, but all three statues I've encountered so far have been
of newts. It occurs to me that I should be able to put them all into a
room with an altar, whisper a secret incantation and turn it into a
newt-temple. Not sure what would be so special about a newt-temple,
but it seems like a sensible thing to do.

Perhaps it could even be a way for the player to generate altars? I
still think nh needs a way to do this, although I'm sure everyone
agrees that if this were to be implemented it would have to be a long,
laborious and dangerous process...


*dogscoff cracks knuckles then stifles an evil laugh*


How about this:



***Spoily*** you have been warned.




1: Gather together blessed statues of a particular monster class (ie
all monsters of the same ascii symbol) in an equilateral square, with
a space between each pair of adjacent statues. Like this:

' ' ' ' '

'       '

'  @    '

'       '

' ' ' ' '

There should be no walls, rocks, water, lava, sinks, toilets,
fountains, altars or doors inside the square. The number of statues
required should be dependent on monster difficulty, so that you need
144 jackal statues for a jackal-temple but only 25 centaur statues for
a centaur-temple, and just 4 statues for a Balrog temple. Lichens,
grid bugs and other too-easy monsters should have requirements that
are impossibly high (ie would require a square bigger than the screen)
Once you have enough statues, entering the square triggers a message
("You feel the as though the [foo]-god is watching you") to let you
know that you are ready for stage two.

2: Bring a live specimen of the monster into the middle of the statues
and #pray. You only have a chance (modified by luck and perhaps other
factors) of success. Needless to say, If you've already annoyed the
foo-god you are trying to invoke, your attempt will fail painfully. If
you fail, your own god gets angry anyway, and might zap a few
lightning bolts about, and/or maybe blast a few of your precious
statues into dust.

3: If the attempt is successful, a temple is created with the square
of statues acting as the temple's "walls". The live specimen you
provided is turned into a priest (only peaceful if the monster it came
from was peaceful or tame), an "altar to the [foo]-God" is created at
the centre of the square and your own God is made made very very
angry.

4: Destruction of any of the temple statues (or, in slashem, a
polymorphed statue reverting to an inappropriate form) will desecrate
the temple and anger both the priest and the foo-god, who will bring
all the (remaining) statues to life to kill you. Unfortunately, due to
the arrangement of statues, you can never force bolt more than one
line of statues at a time so there will always be some left over.

5: Once you've managed to get back in your own God's good books, you
can try to convert the altar to your own needs. Of course, that will
also incur the wrath of priest and the foo-god, who (if they haven't
already done so) will bring the statues to life to kill you.

So let's just recap: I said it should be a long, laborious and
dangerous process.

Long: Well, if you choose to find (and perhaps polypile) the statues
rather than create them, it would certainly take a while. In fact, I
imagine it would be very rare to ever get enough without creating them
yourself, although the oracle level does come with a good number of
guaranteed centaur statues, and I understand some quest levels are
fairly statue-rich.
However, even creating and stoning all the necessary monsters would
take considerable preparation. You might get lucky with the bones file
of someone who tried and failed, I suppose, but that's not going to
happen often. Also, in order to do this you have to seriously anger
your own deity, and getting back into his/her good books would take
time. If you don't care about your god's opinion and you don't want to
convert the altar, then that wouldn't apply, but I imagine most people
would want to get a co-aligned altar. There's always the helm of
OppAlign, of course. I'm not entirely sure how they work, but
presumably you'd wear one at the creation of the altar so that you
opposite god gets angry at you rather than your own, then take it off
to convert the altar immediately. This is a bit of a loophole, but I
imagine it could be either tolerated or closed.

Laborious: Lugging statues about is certainly laborious. I'd say
creating and stoning a few hundred of some non-threatening little
beastie is pretty dull as well. Also, bear in mind that you need to
arrange all the statues in an empty square- so if you're not planning
on doing this in the mines or in a big room, you'll probably need to
do a fair bit of pick-axe work. You could choose a higher-difficulty
monster to reduce the amount of stoning / size of room required, but
in doing so you are trading decreased labour for increased danger.
Then there's the resources required: How many scrolls, markers and
chickens would you need to reverse-genocide and stone 144 floating
eyes? And did I mention that each of the statues has to be blessed?
And statues are too big to stack in inventory, so that's one holy
water per statue... The idea is that by the time you have the
resources to attempt something like this, the chances are you've
already found and converted an altar somewhere anyway- but, nethack
being nethack, there will be some rare cirumstances where you might
just consider it worthwhile to go through all of this. (Come on, I
know plenty of you would give it a go just for the hell of it)

Dangerous: Well, the danger depends very much on the class of monster
you choose for your temple, which- as stated above- has a serious
impact on the amount of labour and resources required.
You are also restricted to higher-level monsters somewhat, because
although it is certainly possible to reverse- genocide 169 newts,
remember that the game will only generate a certain number of each
monster before they become extinct, and unless you've been planning
this all along you've probably killed half a hundred newts already.
Even if you do decide to make a newt-temple, there's still the
temptation to pick up one too many statues, overburden yourself and
fall prey to YASD. Also, remember that rubber chickens can be almost
as dangerous for the chickener as for the chickenee, (particularly
when burdened).
Finally, whatever the class of monster you choose, you may have to
anger your own deity to create an altar, which I'm sure everyone
agrees is not something to be taken lightly. If you then want to
convert your new altar you also have to deal with an angry priest and
a horde of minions.

Anyway, if any of you [s] bones users ever find a stack of cursed
statues atop a grave labelled "dogscoff, killed by a newt while
helpless", you'll know why.

